 level of safety  and i think in this germany has an  enormous interest  you have also criticized the decision i  think  for climate change reasons you're saying  that i i believe  i i haven't if i may uh  correct you i haven't criticized the  decision what i'm saying  because each country is sovereign and  there's no  one size fit all in energy  what we are saying is that nuclear is  scientifically speaking part of the  solution  nuclear is a very extremely low  carbon emitter it's an energy  that it's it's it's dispatchable is  there  can work together with renewables  and the fact is that if you didn't have  nuclear energy imagine  100 reactors in the united states all  that you have in france  everywhere the co2 emissions  would skyrocket immediately so it is a  fact  we have to work together and and find  the solutions  as i have said the goal is to  decarbonize not to denuclearize well  decarbonized denuclearized obviously the  critics in germany would say  nuclear energy might not leave emissions  carbon emissions but  it leaves behind waste that needs to be  dealt with  and that is an equal problem indeed it's  a unique situation  but we do have the technology we do have  the means to deal with this  i'm not minimizing the fact that you  need social acceptability  nobody wants to be close to waste to any  waste  it's things you don't want to do it you  don't want to see it  it's normal the what the iea can do is  to  make sure that technologies are  developed to deal with this in a  sustainable  way there are different different views  we respect them all  the important thing is to compare nodes  and to work together in a future  oriented  perspective mr grossi thank you very  much for talking to deutsche weller  thank you it's been a pleasure it's been  a pleasure  you
 

